diagnosis_text:
The network is healthy; no blackhole at super_spine_router_0. End-to-end tests show pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) reaches pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) with 0% loss (pc_0_0→pc_0_1: rx=2/2, rtt≈0.92 ms; pc_0_1→pc_0_0: rx=2/2, rtt≈0.97 ms). In this CLOS design, traffic between the two leaf subnets does not traverse the “top” box; it goes leaf→spine→leaf. Direct evidence: leaf_router_0_0 routes 10.0.1.0/24 “via 172.16.0.4, eth0” (toward spine_router_0_0). That spine in turn routes 10.0.1.0/24 “via 172.16.0.7, eth2” directly toward leaf_router_0_1, so packets descend immediately to the destination leaf without ever reaching super_spine_router_0.

Super spine control-plane is also healthy: show bgp summary on super_spine_router_0 has both peers up (172.16.0.1 AS 65100, 172.16.0.3 AS 65101) with 2 prefixes received/sent each, and its routing table shows valid next-hops. There is no evidence of drops or misrouting.

Root cause: The reported “black hole at the top” is a misinterpretation of the forwarding path; pc_0_0→pc_0_1 flows do not cross the super spine and are already completing successfully.

Corrective fix: None required. Do not restart super_spine_router_0. If further validation is desired, use traceroute from pc_0_0 to confirm the leaf→spine→leaf path and monitor BGP/links, but the fabric is operating correctly now.
